---
import { BASE } from "../../base";
import { DOCS } from "../../docs";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";

const frontmatter = {
  title: "Documentation",
  description: "How Parousia works, how to install and configure it, and how to write Activities.",
};
---

<Docs frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p>
    Parousia shows what you are doing in your browser on Discord. It has four parts, and each one
    has a single job:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The browser extension</strong> (or a userscript) watches your tabs, finds the
      Activity for the page, and sends what it produced to Desktop.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Parousia Desktop</strong> is a small native program. It accepts connections from
      extensions it recognizes, on your computer only, and passes presence on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A platform adapter</strong> inside Desktop turns presence into what a platform
      expects. Discord is the first; Fluxer and Stoat are planned.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Activities</strong> describe how to read a site: which pages it covers and what to
      say about them. They come from PreMiD and from Parousia's own repository.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Without Desktop, the extension can still reach Discord through the app that
    Discord-RPC-Extension installs; see <a href={`${BASE}docs/compatibility/`}>Compatibility</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Pages</h2>
  <ul>
    {
      DOCS.filter((doc) => doc.slug).map((doc) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`${BASE}docs/${doc.slug}`}>{doc.title}</a>: {doc.summary}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</Docs>
